<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Oldenburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1091 CE – 1774 CE (approx.) · capital Oldenburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A North Sea county whose counts supplied kings to Denmark, Norway and Sweden, raised in 1774 to the Duchy of Oldenburg.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Count Elimar I appears in 1108, and his family built Oldenburg castle on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunte">Hunte</a>. The counts expanded over the marshes towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a> and the Jade, fighting the free Frisian peasants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stedingen">Stedingen</a>, who were crushed in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> in 1234.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In 1448 Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-i-of-denmark">Christian of Oldenburg</a> was elected King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> as Christian I, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-oldenburg">House of Oldenburg</a>, which later ruled Denmark, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in its various branches. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-gunther-count-of-oldenburg">Anton Günther</a> kept Oldenburg out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years' War and was famous for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-breeding">horse breeding</a>. The economy relied on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and dyke-building.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-gunther-count-of-oldenburg">Anton Günther</a> died in 1667, Oldenburg passed to the Danish crown. In 1773 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> exchanged it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, which gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-augustus-i-duke-of-oldenburg">Frederick August</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-holstein-gottorp">Holstein-Gottorp</a>, and in 1774 it was raised to a duchy. It later became a grand duchy and now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-oldenburg.jpg" alt="County of Oldenburg" width="320" height="242"><figcaption>Image: Monsieurlelephant · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Danish_oldenburg_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1091 CE – 1774 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Oldenburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Elimar I, Christian I of Denmark, Anton Günther</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, later Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Oldenburg</dd></dl></aside></div>
